# The protected handler fails, so the client is not charged.
#
# This is the case for selling access to data you do not own. The resource server
# resells an upstream provider, the upstream is down, and the server answers 503 —
# so the payment must not be taken, even though the client already signed and sent
# one.
#
# The flow is what makes that possible. exact uses the authorization flow, verify
# then resource then settle, and a handler answering 400 or above cancels the
# settlement rather than completing it: upstream calls that reason handler_failed,
# and states the guarantee as settling only after the handler returns a successful
# response. The signed transaction is simply never broadcast, so nothing needs
# refunding — which is the whole reason the payment is taken last.
#
# No official x402 example covers this path; its own coverage is unit tests
# asserting the cancellation hook fired and that settlement was never called. Those
# cannot see whether money moved. This can, and the seller's balance is the
# assertion: it is the only way to tell "not settled" from "settled and reported
# badly".
adduser seller

gnoland start

gnokey query bank/balances/$seller_user_addr
stdout '1000000000ugnot'

x402facilitator
x402seller outage.json $seller_user_addr

# The client pays: it gets the 402, signs, and retries. The handler then fails.
exec node $X402_BUYER
stdout '"status":503'

# No settlement was reported, so there is no transaction to name.
stdout '"transaction":""'

# And the money did not move. The client keeps its funds and its fee, because the
# transaction it signed was never broadcast.
gnokey query bank/balances/$seller_user_addr
stdout '1000000000ugnot'
! stdout '1000250000ugnot'

-- outage.json --
{
  "route": "GET /forecast",
  "price": { "asset": "ugnot", "amount": "250000" },
  "description": "Hourly forecast, resold from an upstream provider",
  "mimeType": "application/json",
  "status": 503,
  "body": {
    "error": "upstream provider unavailable"
  }
}
